When you draw one frame,
you can eat one ramen,

00:00:06.521 --> 00:00:08.817
but the rate of their life increase.

00:00:08.817 --> 00:00:09.908
- The inflation.

00:00:09.908 --> 00:00:11.491
- Yeah.

00:00:11.491 --> 00:00:13.489
So now if you want to eat a ramen,

00:00:13.489 --> 00:00:16.531
you have at least to
do five or six frames.

00:00:16.531 --> 00:00:17.364
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:17.364 --> 00:00:20.409
- Am I allowed to ask you
what that might be per frame?

00:00:20.409 --> 00:00:21.908
- Well, when you're in-betweener,

00:00:21.908 --> 00:00:23.408
you are paid like-

00:00:23.408 --> 00:00:25.997
(chill music plays)

00:00:25.997 --> 00:00:28.382
- Hello and welcome to another
episode of "Trash Taste,"

00:00:28.382 --> 00:00:31.773
the anime podcast, where
we talk about Japan,

00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:35.221
food, shitting, and occasionally anime.

00:00:35.221 --> 00:00:36.718
I was trying to remember
what I said last week.

00:00:36.718 --> 00:00:37.915
(everyone laughs)

00:00:37.915 --> 00:00:39.329
- Is that what you said last week?

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:40.979
- I think that's what I said last week.

00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:43.466
I am your host for today, Garnt again.

00:00:43.466 --> 00:00:45.842
And joining me is as usual the boys.

00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:49.910
And once again, we have
a guest as I'm hosting.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.546
We have a Ken Arto.

00:00:51.546 --> 00:00:53.814
Do you want to introduce
yourself and what you do?

00:00:53.814 --> 00:00:54.772
- Yes.

00:00:54.772 --> 00:00:55.605
Nice to meet you.

00:00:55.605 --> 00:00:56.505
My name is Ken Arto.

00:00:56.505 --> 00:00:59.838
I am a key animator in Japan since 2015.

00:01:00.713 --> 00:01:03.718
And so I had work on projects

00:01:03.718 --> 00:01:05.656
like "Dragon Ball Super Broly,"

00:01:05.656 --> 00:01:08.683
"My Hero Academia,"
"JoJo," a lot of things.

00:01:08.683 --> 00:01:09.516
- [Connor] Yes.

00:01:09.516 --> 00:01:11.099
- And-

00:01:11.099 --> 00:01:13.808
- Sorry, I just love "JoJo."

00:01:13.808 --> 00:01:17.767
- And I am a key animator at Studio 4C.

00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:18.600
- Oh, okay.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.433
Wow.

00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:20.888
Well, we have a quick spread

00:01:20.888 --> 00:01:22.341
of some of the shows you've worked on.

00:01:22.341 --> 00:01:23.174
Not everything.

00:01:23.174 --> 00:01:24.527
- [Joey] Wow, you worked
on "Monsters University?"

00:01:24.527 --> 00:01:26.437
(everyone laughs)

00:01:26.437 --> 00:01:27.270
- Yeah.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.038
I like pretty much shonen jump, so yeah.

00:01:30.038 --> 00:01:31.810
- Yeah, I mean, obviously
this isn't all the shows

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.569
that Ken's worked on, but you know,

00:01:33.569 --> 00:01:36.041
these are probably I'd
say the most notable ones.

00:01:36.041 --> 00:01:38.028
I mean, there's obviously
more like, you know,

00:01:38.028 --> 00:01:39.432
the recent "Jujutsu Kaisen."

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:40.390
- "Jujutsu Kaisen."

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:41.424
- Episode 16, right?

00:01:41.424 --> 00:01:42.257
- 16.

00:01:42.257 --> 00:01:43.991
That would be this Friday.

00:01:43.991 --> 00:01:47.042
And recently also
"Kimetsu No Yaiba Movie."

00:01:47.042 --> 00:01:48.125
- Oh my God.

00:01:48.125 --> 00:01:49.708
Just casually drop that.

00:01:49.708 --> 00:01:50.982
- That's one hell of a CV.

00:01:50.982 --> 00:01:52.448
Well, thank you very much for coming on

00:01:52.448 --> 00:01:54.168
because it's nice to have someone

00:01:54.168 --> 00:01:56.300
who's actually been
working in the industry

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.157
and is knowledgeable

00:01:57.157 --> 00:01:59.426
about what it's actually
like working in there

00:01:59.426 --> 00:02:01.955
instead of just having three
guys sitting in the room

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:04.354
just taking guesses, taking pot shots.

00:02:04.354 --> 00:02:06.272
- We just take, you know,
knowledgeable guesses.

00:02:06.272 --> 00:02:09.026
Sometimes not knowledgeable guesses.

00:02:09.026 --> 00:02:10.365
- I think when we don't have a guest,

00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:12.128
it's like don't listen to what we say.

00:02:12.128 --> 00:02:13.395
We don't know what we're talking about.

00:02:13.395 --> 00:02:17.730
- But now we're literally
like first hand source.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.253
- Now we can get the answers

00:02:19.253 --> 00:02:21.127
at the same time as our audience.

00:02:21.127 --> 00:02:24.527
- I am curious if there was,
you know, perhaps one scene

00:02:24.527 --> 00:02:26.323
or one show that you would recommend

00:02:26.323 --> 00:02:28.872
for someone to watch to maybe
experience your work the best,

00:02:28.872 --> 00:02:30.319
what would you point them towards?

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:32.316
- Actually I would maybe say,

00:02:32.316 --> 00:02:35.547
I'm working on a movie for Studio 4C.

00:02:35.547 --> 00:02:39.202
I can't say the name 'cause it's not.

00:02:39.202 --> 00:02:43.407
Actually, yeah, this
movie I put all my guts,

00:02:43.407 --> 00:02:47.157
and so yes, you have to
wait a bit, but yeah.

00:02:49.937 --> 00:02:52.107
- That was the most slick
way to promote the new movie.

00:02:52.107 --> 00:02:53.315
(everyone laughs)

00:02:53.315 --> 00:02:55.061
"My best work is one
that you're gonna have

00:02:55.061 --> 00:02:56.409
"to go see in cinemas."

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:58.272
(everyone laughs)

00:02:58.272 --> 00:02:59.995
- Yeah, "Jujutsu Kaisen" episode 16,

00:02:59.995 --> 00:03:03.817
I think it's a good shot I have done so.

00:03:03.817 --> 00:03:04.880
- I mean, how long have you been working

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.588
in the industry now?

00:03:06.588 --> 00:03:09.701
- So, we don't count my internship,

00:03:09.701 --> 00:03:11.488
it will be now five years.

00:03:11.488 --> 00:03:13.910
And so I start as in-betweener.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.259
So to...

00:03:15.259 --> 00:03:16.092
- Between frames, right?

00:03:16.092 --> 00:03:16.925
- Between frames.

00:03:16.925 --> 00:03:18.944
Clean the in-betweens.

00:03:18.944 --> 00:03:22.473
And I have done just
for one year and half.

00:03:22.473 --> 00:03:25.176
And after I have switch of schedule,

00:03:25.176 --> 00:03:27.000
I went to Yapiko Animation.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.558
And the previous was at Graphinica.

00:03:29.558 --> 00:03:34.008
And Yapiko, Yapiko I made
so two year and half.

00:03:34.008 --> 00:03:37.577
And after Yapiko, now I am at Studio 4C.

00:03:37.577 --> 00:03:39.285
- [Connor] Studio 4C.

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.368
- 'Cause like its,

00:03:40.368 --> 00:03:43.172
'cause your background is
that you're half Japanese,

00:03:43.172 --> 00:03:44.202
half French, right?

00:03:44.202 --> 00:03:45.160
- Yeah.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.785
- So was like, what made
you want to start working

00:03:47.785 --> 00:03:49.942
in the anime industry?

00:03:49.942 --> 00:03:53.275
- Actually in France, we have in the 90s

00:03:55.474 --> 00:03:58.927
and a show called "Club Dorothee."

00:03:58.927 --> 00:04:01.030
And actually, Dorothee was a woman

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.958
who bought a lot of anime.

00:04:03.958 --> 00:04:07.911
And "Dragon Ball," "Saint
Seiya," "Maison Ikkoku," "Ranma."

00:04:07.911 --> 00:04:11.592
And so when I was little,
I watched those shows,

00:04:11.592 --> 00:04:16.040
and also I went often
to Japan with my mother.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.978
And yeah, I was used to watch
a lot of anime when I was kid.

00:04:19.978 --> 00:04:23.652
And I grew with manga also, and so, yeah,

00:04:23.652 --> 00:04:27.518
I used to draw those
characters when I was little.

00:04:27.518 --> 00:04:31.018
And so, at first I wanted to be a mangaka.

00:04:31.877 --> 00:04:33.657
- As does everyone I think.

00:04:33.657 --> 00:04:35.517
I think every weeb has had that dream

00:04:35.517 --> 00:04:37.051
one time in their lives.

00:04:37.051 --> 00:04:38.509
- No.

00:04:38.509 --> 00:04:41.739
- Every weeb who draws their
first anime character's like,

00:04:41.739 --> 00:04:44.042
"Yeah, I'm on my way
to be on shonen jump."

00:04:44.042 --> 00:04:45.139
(everyone laughs)

00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:46.805
"Let's go."
- Are you telling me, Connor,

00:04:46.805 --> 00:04:48.942
that you've never had like a story

00:04:48.942 --> 00:04:51.784
or something that you've
just like, fantasized about?

00:04:51.784 --> 00:04:52.742
- No.

00:04:52.742 --> 00:04:55.138
- You never fantasized as a kid at all?

00:04:55.138 --> 00:04:55.971
- Probably.

00:04:55.971 --> 00:04:57.733
But I probably, that was
like my thing, right?

00:04:57.733 --> 00:04:59.592
I'm not sharing it with other people.

00:04:59.592 --> 00:05:01.342
It's my story, you know what I mean?

00:05:01.342 --> 00:05:02.300
I'm not sharing it.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.044
- You never had a phase
where you were like,

00:05:04.044 --> 00:05:06.943
oh, I guess though like you
started watching anime, like,

00:05:06.943 --> 00:05:08.521
a little bit of like an older stage.

00:05:08.521 --> 00:05:09.354
- That's true.

00:05:09.354 --> 00:05:12.545
- 15, 16 I started watching
anime. So pretty late I think.

00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:14.763
I probably didn't get
that whimsical phase of-

00:05:14.763 --> 00:05:16.084
- Yeah 'cause I mean, how
old were you when you, like,

00:05:16.084 --> 00:05:17.512
first, like, really, I guess like,

00:05:17.512 --> 00:05:19.727
while knowing that this is anime?

00:05:19.727 --> 00:05:21.439
- Oh, I don't remember.

00:05:21.439 --> 00:05:24.272
I think the shock I had was in '98

00:05:25.746 --> 00:05:29.034
when I watch "Princess Mononoke."

00:05:29.034 --> 00:05:29.904
- Oh yeah, yeah.

00:05:29.904 --> 00:05:31.487
- And for the same,

00:05:32.591 --> 00:05:35.885
I didn't know what I'm
watching, but it was beautiful.

00:05:35.885 --> 00:05:37.881
Scary and beautiful.

00:05:37.881 --> 00:05:40.283
And I was just nine.

00:05:40.283 --> 00:05:41.366
- Just nine.

00:05:41.366 --> 00:05:42.199
- Yeah, just nine.

00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:46.675
And also when I watch
"Evangelion" I was just 10 or 11.

00:05:46.675 --> 00:05:48.133
- What?

00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:49.726
Did you understand anything?

00:05:49.726 --> 00:05:51.245
- Oh, hey, same here actually.

00:05:51.245 --> 00:05:52.078
Same.

00:05:52.078 --> 00:05:52.911
I'm glad somebody-

00:05:52.911 --> 00:05:54.911
- I don't know if I was
understanding everything,

00:05:54.911 --> 00:05:57.422
but oh, there is mecha, there is alien.

00:05:57.422 --> 00:05:58.645
Okay, they have to beat them.

00:05:58.645 --> 00:06:01.595
(everyone laughs)

00:06:01.595 --> 00:06:03.919
- That's all you, like,
understand about "Eva"

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:04.783
when you're like 10.

00:06:04.783 --> 00:06:07.377
It's like good guys, bad guys.

00:06:07.377 --> 00:06:08.585
- I mean, I'll be honest.

00:06:08.585 --> 00:06:09.656
I watched it last year

00:06:09.656 --> 00:06:11.301
and that's pretty much my understanding.

00:06:11.301 --> 00:06:12.259
(everyone laughs)

00:06:12.259 --> 00:06:17.105
- Also the character was 14
on the story and I was 11 so-

00:06:17.105 --> 00:06:17.938
- Close enough, right?

00:06:17.938 --> 00:06:18.771
- Yeah, close enough.

00:06:18.771 --> 00:06:19.605
- [Joey] Yeah, true, true.

00:06:19.605 --> 00:06:20.438
- Okay, I can pilot.

00:06:20.438 --> 00:06:21.630
- It's gonna be me.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:22.917
- Maybe I can be in the EVA.

00:06:22.917 --> 00:06:24.864
- I actually have a similar
story about like "Eva"

00:06:24.864 --> 00:06:28.493
'cause "Eva" is the second
proper anime that I watched

00:06:28.493 --> 00:06:30.142
after discovering it was anime.

00:06:30.142 --> 00:06:32.863
So, the first anime I ever watched

00:06:32.863 --> 00:06:35.050
knowing it was an anime was "Naruto."

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:38.420
And how I found that was I
was on a random gaming forum,

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:40.010
and you know, it was back

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:43.924
when having anime avatars
was actually cool.

00:06:43.924 --> 00:06:45.254
(everyone laughs)

00:06:45.254 --> 00:06:46.087
You know?

00:06:46.087 --> 00:06:49.261
And I was like, "What are
these avatars that people have?

00:06:49.261 --> 00:06:50.253
"They look really cool."

00:06:50.253 --> 00:06:53.221
And I found that one guy had this avatar

00:06:53.221 --> 00:06:55.182
that was from the show called "Naruto."

00:06:55.182 --> 00:06:57.617
And I'm like, "Oh, this kind
of looks like Dragon Ball Z."

00:06:57.617 --> 00:06:59.840
Watched "Naruto," fucking loved it.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.504
Introduced me to anime properly.

00:07:02.504 --> 00:07:04.407
And then I went on the same gaming forum,

00:07:04.407 --> 00:07:05.890
and I made it like an anime thread.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:09.191
I was like, "Guys, this
Naruto thing is awesome.

00:07:09.191 --> 00:07:11.015
"Can you guys recommend
to me something else

00:07:11.015 --> 00:07:12.329
"to watch like Naruto?"

00:07:12.329 --> 00:07:14.873
And some guy deadass goes,

00:07:14.873 --> 00:07:16.621
"You should watch Evangelion.

00:07:16.621 --> 00:07:18.348
"Totally like Naruto."

00:07:18.348 --> 00:07:19.181
- Totally.

00:07:19.181 --> 00:07:20.784
- So, I went into "Evangelion,"

00:07:20.784 --> 00:07:22.602
I was like an 11, 12 year old,

00:07:22.602 --> 00:07:24.159
thinking it was a shonen anime.

00:07:24.159 --> 00:07:24.992
- Oh my God.

00:07:24.992 --> 00:07:28.705
- And so for like the
first I'd say half of it,

00:07:28.705 --> 00:07:33.491
it could like maybe have
like passed as a shonen

00:07:33.491 --> 00:07:35.564
because, you know, Shinji
could have still, you know,

00:07:35.564 --> 00:07:36.926
Shinji was still doing stuff.

00:07:36.926 --> 00:07:38.815
And then the halfway point hit,

00:07:38.815 --> 00:07:41.686
and then everyone just
got sad and depressed.

00:07:41.686 --> 00:07:43.922
And my 11 year old mind was just like,

00:07:43.922 --> 00:07:45.774
"Why are people like this?

00:07:45.774 --> 00:07:47.648
"This is so stupid.

00:07:47.648 --> 00:07:49.205
"This is so unrealistic."

00:07:49.205 --> 00:07:51.334
I remember thinking as an 11 year old,

00:07:51.334 --> 00:07:53.987
the writing of the show is so bad

00:07:53.987 --> 00:07:56.938
because the adults don't
have their shit together.

00:07:56.938 --> 00:07:57.900
Why is that?

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.196
So why don't the adults
have their shit together?

00:08:00.196 --> 00:08:01.218
This is so unrealistic.

00:08:01.218 --> 00:08:02.981
- Imagine, like, 11 year old Garnt going

00:08:02.981 --> 00:08:05.120
up to his mom being like,
"Mother, what is depression?"

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:07.102
(everyone laughs)

00:08:07.102 --> 00:08:09.616
"I learnt it in this
show called Evangelion."

00:08:09.616 --> 00:08:12.335
- And now I'm just like,
"Oh yeah, so adults just,

00:08:12.335 --> 00:08:13.821
"no adults just have their together.

00:08:13.821 --> 00:08:15.404
"That's just life."

00:08:16.516 --> 00:08:18.016
- So you've always been
able to speak Japanese

00:08:18.016 --> 00:08:18.974
since a young age?

00:08:18.974 --> 00:08:20.553
- Yes, it was my mother.

00:08:20.553 --> 00:08:22.878
And so, yes, I learned,

00:08:22.878 --> 00:08:25.446
I don't know if I can say I learned-

00:08:25.446 --> 00:08:26.392
- But you just know it?

00:08:26.392 --> 00:08:28.695
- Yeah, I just know.

00:08:28.695 --> 00:08:30.689
With anime, with drama, with movies,

00:08:30.689 --> 00:08:34.354
and my mother and some of my family.

00:08:34.354 --> 00:08:38.169
Like I said, it's a family language.

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:41.069
So when you have to work,

00:08:41.069 --> 00:08:45.227
you use another Japanese
language which is keigo.

00:08:45.227 --> 00:08:46.060
- [Connor] Oh God.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.518
- And so yes-

00:08:47.518 --> 00:08:49.581
- Keigo is like very formal Japanese?

00:08:49.581 --> 00:08:50.414
- Very formal.

00:08:50.414 --> 00:08:53.994
When you have to spoke with
your senpai, with your boss.

00:08:53.994 --> 00:08:54.952
It's different.

00:08:54.952 --> 00:08:58.558
You have to put your
interacter up and you're down.

00:08:58.558 --> 00:09:01.222
- Basically it's the bane of every, like,

00:09:01.222 --> 00:09:03.765
person trying to learn Japanese.

00:09:03.765 --> 00:09:06.550
It's smooth sailing until you hit keigo.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:07.510
And then everyone's like, "I give up."

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:08.563
- Smooth sailing until you hit keigo?

00:09:08.563 --> 00:09:09.396
- Oh no, trust me.

00:09:09.396 --> 00:09:10.229
- Oh, fuck off.

00:09:10.229 --> 00:09:12.297
- If you think it's hard
now, wait 'til you hit keigo.

00:09:12.297 --> 00:09:14.617
- I've just given up on the
thought of ever learning keigo.

00:09:14.617 --> 00:09:15.450
I'm like, keigo?

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:16.283
I'm okay.

00:09:16.283 --> 00:09:17.568
- Because I thought that as well.

00:09:17.568 --> 00:09:19.275
'Cause I kind of was the
same thing where I'd like,

00:09:19.275 --> 00:09:21.036
you know, I don't speak
keigo to my parents.

00:09:21.036 --> 00:09:22.144
I don't think any kid does.

00:09:22.144 --> 00:09:26.447
So I had to learn keigo on
the job when I first started,

00:09:26.447 --> 00:09:29.257
like, you know, part-time jobs in Japan.

00:09:29.257 --> 00:09:32.009
And I was like, I thought I knew Japanese.

00:09:32.009 --> 00:09:34.520
I'm told I had to start using keigo.

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:36.942
It just threw every
conception about Japanese

00:09:36.942 --> 00:09:37.786
out the window.

00:09:37.786 --> 00:09:39.412
- Is there like any comparison

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:42.446
that's even close to English with keigo?

00:09:42.446 --> 00:09:44.282
- It's like, oh man.

00:09:44.282 --> 00:09:45.782
I don't know, is there a comparison?

00:09:45.782 --> 00:09:49.682
- Would it be like trying
to speak a very old,

00:09:49.682 --> 00:09:52.112
like, very posh kind
of version of English?

00:09:52.112 --> 00:09:56.890
- It's kinda like going from
only learning internet slang

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:58.573
to learning Shakespeare.

00:09:58.573 --> 00:09:59.494
- I see.

00:09:59.494 --> 00:10:01.109
- It's basically another language.

00:10:01.109 --> 00:10:03.480
- So you motherfuckers have
to learn to speak in cursive.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:04.677
- Pretty much, yeah.

00:10:04.677 --> 00:10:06.927
(everyone laughs)

00:10:06.927 --> 00:10:09.056
We literally like, it's like
a Shakespeare play, you know,

00:10:09.056 --> 00:10:11.279
when the translation is on the page.

00:10:11.279 --> 00:10:12.142
It's basically that.

00:10:12.142 --> 00:10:13.366
(everyone laughs)

00:10:13.366 --> 00:10:15.840
- I saw there was like Japanese cursive.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:16.673
- Is there?

00:10:16.673 --> 00:10:18.544
- Like, yeah, I saw that
someone was writing Japanese

00:10:18.544 --> 00:10:19.377
in cursive.

00:10:19.377 --> 00:10:20.905
- Oh, maybe like calligraphy, right?

00:10:20.905 --> 00:10:21.738
- No, no, no.

00:10:21.738 --> 00:10:22.571
Like, legit.

00:10:22.571 --> 00:10:24.271
Like, it was like a doctor's
note someone had showed me

00:10:24.271 --> 00:10:25.104
and it was-

00:10:25.104 --> 00:10:25.937
- Oh no, dude.

00:10:25.937 --> 00:10:26.770
Every time I have to
go to the doctors here,

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:29.113
I'm just like, I might as well die

00:10:29.113 --> 00:10:30.524
'cause I can't read this note.

00:10:30.524 --> 00:10:32.456
- It's good to know that
that transcends language,

00:10:32.456 --> 00:10:34.160
that doctors just can't write for shit.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:35.694
(everyone laughs)

00:10:35.694 --> 00:10:37.027
Somehow that's-

00:10:37.027 --> 00:10:37.860
- It's universal.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.565
- Yeah, and I thought kanji was hard,

00:10:39.565 --> 00:10:41.859
but you throw in cursive
kanji and I'm like, come on.

00:10:41.859 --> 00:10:43.948
- It's like level 100 comprehension.

00:10:43.948 --> 00:10:45.922
(everyone laughs)

00:10:45.922 --> 00:10:48.250
- Actually when you have some animators,

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:50.734
you have some notes of my sempai on paper.

00:10:50.734 --> 00:10:52.151
I try to read it.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:54.868
(everyone laughs)

00:10:54.868 --> 00:10:57.032
Because what did he?

00:10:57.032 --> 00:10:58.229
I don't.

00:10:58.229 --> 00:10:59.888
- It's like decoding it.

00:10:59.888 --> 00:11:02.792
- I have to decode but
it takes too much time,

00:11:02.792 --> 00:11:06.015
so I'll go to my senpai,
"Sorry, what did you write?"

00:11:06.015 --> 00:11:07.012
- "What the fuck is this?"

00:11:07.012 --> 00:11:08.048
- It's just easier to ask, isn't it?

00:11:08.048 --> 00:11:08.881
- Yeah.

00:11:08.881 --> 00:11:10.763
- So was it anime that,

00:11:10.763 --> 00:11:11.627
I'm guessing it was anime,

00:11:11.627 --> 00:11:15.286
the reason you started to
learn animation and really-

00:11:15.286 --> 00:11:16.869
- At first I think,

00:11:17.744 --> 00:11:22.726
so, I wanted to be mangaka,
but I was not good at school.

00:11:22.726 --> 00:11:24.871
(everyone laughs)

00:11:24.871 --> 00:11:26.264
Not good at school.

00:11:26.264 --> 00:11:30.620
And well, you have lessons,
but I didn't listen to lesson.

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:33.504
I was drawing manga on my notes.

00:11:33.504 --> 00:11:37.754
And so I went to another
school, not a high school.

00:11:38.993 --> 00:11:41.948
A high school where you could
learn about communication,

00:11:41.948 --> 00:11:43.531
art craft-

00:11:43.531 --> 00:11:44.688
- This is in France, right?

00:11:44.688 --> 00:11:45.521
- Yeah, in France.

00:11:45.521 --> 00:11:47.880
I was born and raised in France.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:51.163
And at first it was about communication,

00:11:51.163 --> 00:11:53.629
how to do commercial.

00:11:53.629 --> 00:11:55.817
And it was not pleasant,

00:11:55.817 --> 00:12:00.150
but I had to go this way
to go to animation schools.

00:12:01.631 --> 00:12:02.722
- Right, right, right.

00:12:02.722 --> 00:12:05.641
- So I take to baccalaureat in French.

00:12:05.641 --> 00:12:07.445
So first degree I think.

00:12:07.445 --> 00:12:08.278
- Oh, bachelor.

00:12:08.278 --> 00:12:09.236
- Bachelor, yes.

00:12:09.236 --> 00:12:12.075
- So you did a bachelor's
degree in animation.

00:12:12.075 --> 00:12:12.908
- Bachelor.

00:12:12.908 --> 00:12:16.661
And after I went to animation school.

00:12:16.661 --> 00:12:17.869
- Oh, wow.

00:12:17.869 --> 00:12:20.555
So, how did that transition
from that in France

00:12:20.555 --> 00:12:21.845
to being in Japan?

00:12:21.845 --> 00:12:25.604
'Cause I feel like that's
like a big leap, right?

00:12:25.604 --> 00:12:28.771
- When you're at art school in France,

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:33.052
in France anime wasn't,

00:12:33.052 --> 00:12:34.281
it was popular,

00:12:34.281 --> 00:12:37.623
but for adults, it's what kind of-

00:12:37.623 --> 00:12:39.435
- What kind of child
nonsense is this, yeah.

00:12:39.435 --> 00:12:41.873
- This and, "Oh, you
should change some style.

00:12:41.873 --> 00:12:43.254
"It's not a good style."

00:12:43.254 --> 00:12:44.555
(everyone laughs)

00:12:44.555 --> 00:12:48.305
You have others who
watching you from up and,

00:12:50.234 --> 00:12:51.681
"Change the style.

00:12:51.681 --> 00:12:53.364
"You're drawing too much manga.

00:12:53.364 --> 00:12:56.707
"It's not that bad, but it's manga."

00:12:56.707 --> 00:12:58.790
And so, yeah, I was okay.

00:13:01.573 --> 00:13:03.433
If I draw manga, it's bad things.

00:13:03.433 --> 00:13:05.705
So I changed my style.

00:13:05.705 --> 00:13:09.455
But I actually, I wanted
to draw manga style.

00:13:11.122 --> 00:13:14.827
- I feel like in Europe, there
is the notion of animation is

00:13:14.827 --> 00:13:17.549
especially anime looks for children.

00:13:17.549 --> 00:13:18.827
Because I feel like everyone I spoke to

00:13:18.827 --> 00:13:20.783
who grew up in Europe seems
that way where it's like,

00:13:20.783 --> 00:13:24.424
"Oh, after I'm 16 I can't
watch this child stuff."

00:13:24.424 --> 00:13:25.507
- Yeah.

00:13:25.507 --> 00:13:27.124
- Every art student I know, like,

00:13:27.124 --> 00:13:29.471
I have a cousin who's an art student,

00:13:29.471 --> 00:13:30.955
and she loves anime.

00:13:30.955 --> 00:13:32.713
But as soon as she started art school,

00:13:32.713 --> 00:13:33.980
you were just like,

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:37.770
it was looked down upon to
really draw an anime art style.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:38.992
So I don't know if it's still the same-

00:13:38.992 --> 00:13:41.520
- Weebs getting bullied
everywhere they go.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:42.353
- Exactly.

00:13:42.353 --> 00:13:43.686
'Cause like you draw anime,

00:13:43.686 --> 00:13:45.613
and your your art teacher would
just be like, "No, no, no.

00:13:45.613 --> 00:13:47.631
"Please, draw real art."

00:13:47.631 --> 00:13:50.048
- Some arts professor of arts school.

00:13:50.048 --> 00:13:51.172
"Oh, you drew a manga.

00:13:51.172 --> 00:13:55.804
"Okay, I will give you
a bad note, bad score."

00:13:55.804 --> 00:13:56.687
- You have to change.

00:13:56.687 --> 00:14:00.461
- Or draw some American comics, good.

00:14:00.461 --> 00:14:03.045
(everyone laughs)

00:14:03.045 --> 00:14:07.795
So yes, it is not some equals
by some professor, teacher.

00:14:08.648 --> 00:14:12.398
So yeah, it was kind of
harder the first time

00:14:13.455 --> 00:14:15.408
because you are a bit lost.

00:14:15.408 --> 00:14:17.717
"Okay, I have to change my style."

00:14:17.717 --> 00:14:22.467
But when you met other people
who like what you like too,

00:14:23.832 --> 00:14:26.293
you have a better communication,

00:14:26.293 --> 00:14:28.944
and you know, I want to do this

00:14:28.944 --> 00:14:31.100
I want to draw anime style.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:32.020
- I wanna be a weeb.

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:33.703
(everyone laughs)

00:14:33.703 --> 00:14:36.491
- I want to follow my dreams.

00:14:36.491 --> 00:14:37.677
- And so, yeah.

00:14:37.677 --> 00:14:41.208
And I found that, there's an
animation school in France.

00:14:41.208 --> 00:14:44.974
And so I attempt to, I
went to animation school.

00:14:44.974 --> 00:14:47.617
But you rely on Disney way.

00:14:47.617 --> 00:14:48.974
- Right, right, right.

00:14:48.974 --> 00:14:53.591
- Not Japanese way because
Japanese way is very particular

00:14:53.591 --> 00:14:57.258
where you use economy,
economy of the frame.

00:14:59.913 --> 00:15:03.901
Very smart, it's a smart way
to use a Japanese animation.

00:15:03.901 --> 00:15:05.899
In Disney you have a lot of budget,

00:15:05.899 --> 00:15:07.564
so you can put a lot of frames.

00:15:07.564 --> 00:15:09.572
- [Joey] Right, right, right.

00:15:09.572 --> 00:15:11.912
- Throw money at the problem.

00:15:11.912 --> 00:15:13.989
- So basically Disney
is just more drawings

00:15:13.989 --> 00:15:18.137
where the Japanese animation
technique is more, let's say-

00:15:18.137 --> 00:15:18.970
- Optimized?

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:20.303
(everyone laughs)

00:15:20.303 --> 00:15:22.328
- I was looking for the right word.

00:15:22.328 --> 00:15:25.126
- Because also Japanese
animation industry,

00:15:25.126 --> 00:15:28.223
you have to do one episode per week.

00:15:28.223 --> 00:15:31.056
So maybe in America, they finished

00:15:32.337 --> 00:15:34.732
and they delivered the
episodes to the channel.

00:15:34.732 --> 00:15:39.659
But in Japan when you broadcast
the show, it's not finished.

00:15:39.659 --> 00:15:41.221
You're trying to work out.

00:15:41.221 --> 00:15:42.102
- Yeah, 'cause I've noticed

00:15:42.102 --> 00:15:45.273
that a lot of blu-ray
releases have like retouches.

00:15:45.273 --> 00:15:46.888
Like, "Jojo" I remember part four,

00:15:46.888 --> 00:15:49.223
there's some famous
examples of the side by side

00:15:49.223 --> 00:15:50.847
where you can like,
there's a website I think

00:15:50.847 --> 00:15:53.104
where you can sweep the frame across.

00:15:53.104 --> 00:15:55.484
And like some of the character
designs are totally changed

00:15:55.484 --> 00:15:56.594
in the blu-ray.

00:15:56.594 --> 00:15:58.267
- Yeah, that's called DVD retakes.

00:15:58.267 --> 00:16:01.767
And actually when you saw your show on TV.

00:16:03.497 --> 00:16:07.707
And so for people who want to buy the DVD,

00:16:07.707 --> 00:16:10.648
that is a plus and the plus, it's a bonus,

00:16:10.648 --> 00:16:13.828
is a better drawings or
the animation redone.

00:16:13.828 --> 00:16:14.842
- More polished.

00:16:14.842 --> 00:16:17.889
- So, is it like the original
drawing is thrown out

00:16:17.889 --> 00:16:18.855
and they just remake it

00:16:18.855 --> 00:16:20.556
or do they improve the original drawing?

00:16:20.556 --> 00:16:22.891
- Improve or sometimes they redo it.

00:16:22.891 --> 00:16:23.724
- Oh, wow.

00:16:23.724 --> 00:16:25.557
- So it's quite tense.

00:16:26.432 --> 00:16:30.275
- Oh, we can't do a 100
person like we wanted.

00:16:30.275 --> 00:16:31.775
So, let's redo it.

00:16:33.493 --> 00:16:34.330
Oh, this one,

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:38.601
I just mess up on the eyes
or let's redraw the eyes.

00:16:38.601 --> 00:16:39.486
- Right, right, right.

00:16:39.486 --> 00:16:40.569
Wow.

00:16:40.569 --> 00:16:42.842
- I did find that funny
actually, because like, yeah,

00:16:42.842 --> 00:16:45.524
I noticed that like, 'cause, you know,

00:16:45.524 --> 00:16:47.942
when I did voice work for "Pop Team Epic"

00:16:47.942 --> 00:16:50.285
I did it on episode nine.

00:16:50.285 --> 00:16:52.177
And when I got the script for it,

00:16:52.177 --> 00:16:54.442
episode one had already aired,

00:16:54.442 --> 00:16:57.198
so I was like, "Is this
gonna be out in time?"

00:16:57.198 --> 00:16:58.366
(Connor laughs)

00:16:58.366 --> 00:17:01.329
'Cause I always thought it worked like how

00:17:01.329 --> 00:17:02.447
in American TV shows

00:17:02.447 --> 00:17:04.626
where it's like the entire
season is already done.

00:17:04.626 --> 00:17:07.339
It's done and then they
start releasing it.

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:09.011
But in Japan they're just like,
"Nope, we got to do it now."

00:17:09.011 --> 00:17:10.594
It's all real time.

00:17:11.478 --> 00:17:12.561
- It depends.

00:17:12.561 --> 00:17:15.561
When the first episode is broadcast,

00:17:16.724 --> 00:17:21.474
actually so you have might
have done already 10 episodes.

00:17:23.302 --> 00:17:25.538
If the schedule was good.

00:17:25.538 --> 00:17:28.008
Or if they have done just four episode,

00:17:28.008 --> 00:17:29.076
it would be harder.

00:17:29.076 --> 00:17:30.259
(everyone laughs)

00:17:30.259 --> 00:17:32.507
- I love how 10 episodes is
considered a good schedule.

00:17:32.507 --> 00:17:33.340
- Yeah, exactly.

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:35.975
Have you ever been
working up to the deadline

00:17:35.975 --> 00:17:37.237
where you've been working on the show

00:17:37.237 --> 00:17:40.154
and it's been airing at that point?

00:17:41.493 --> 00:17:44.235
- It was for "Hero Aca."

00:17:44.235 --> 00:17:45.985
I think season three.

00:17:47.982 --> 00:17:49.672
I was doing my genga.

00:17:49.672 --> 00:17:52.626
So, yeah, a genga is the second
step of the key animation.

00:17:52.626 --> 00:17:53.853
And-

00:17:53.853 --> 00:17:55.676
- What does genga involve?

00:17:55.676 --> 00:17:57.134
- Okay.

00:17:57.134 --> 00:18:00.190
- Connor knows nothing about animation,

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:01.272
so we're gonna break it down.

00:18:01.272 --> 00:18:02.964
- For now I will explain the process

00:18:02.964 --> 00:18:04.393
of the fabrication of genga.

00:18:04.393 --> 00:18:07.143
So you have the key animator, me,

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:12.360
I received an offer of
work by Twitter or by mail.

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:13.226
- By Twitter?

00:18:13.226 --> 00:18:14.542
- Sometimes by Twitter.

00:18:14.542 --> 00:18:17.401
Because a lot of assistant
production looking

00:18:17.401 --> 00:18:19.079
for animators on Twitter.

00:18:19.079 --> 00:18:21.680
And so for Japan, most
of the Japanese animator

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:25.097
they use Twitter as a portfolio, contact.

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:28.143
- Tons of DM, right?

00:18:28.143 --> 00:18:29.601
Okay, that makes sense.

00:18:29.601 --> 00:18:32.601
- And so, "Hi, I'm from this studio,

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:35.085
"I have this project.

00:18:35.085 --> 00:18:37.887
"The schedule is that, do
you want to work on it?"

00:18:37.887 --> 00:18:39.511
So I look my schedule.

00:18:39.511 --> 00:18:42.224
If it's okay, if it's
good match, "Yeah, sure."

00:18:42.224 --> 00:18:45.724
"Okay, let's put a date for the meetings."

00:18:48.494 --> 00:18:50.181
So okay, this date.

00:18:50.181 --> 00:18:54.921
So I go to the schedule,
meet at the place they said,

00:18:54.921 --> 00:18:58.395
and assistant production who I send mails,

00:18:58.395 --> 00:19:03.202
and we are doing the meeting
for the sequence I have choose.

00:19:03.202 --> 00:19:05.694
At first, they send me a storyboard.

00:19:05.694 --> 00:19:10.487
A storyboard is a little
comic, technical comic about-

00:19:10.487 --> 00:19:11.694
- [Connor] It's like a rough outline

00:19:11.694 --> 00:19:12.868
of how the scene would go, right?

00:19:12.868 --> 00:19:13.701
- Yes.

00:19:13.701 --> 00:19:16.659
With the camera walks, technical notes.

00:19:16.659 --> 00:19:18.303
And I choose my sequence.

00:19:18.303 --> 00:19:21.616
A sequence can be just like three cut,

00:19:21.616 --> 00:19:23.033
30 cuts, 19 cuts.

00:19:24.596 --> 00:19:26.322
- What's a cut?

00:19:26.322 --> 00:19:27.349
- A cut is one scene.

00:19:27.349 --> 00:19:28.182
- Like one scene,

00:19:28.182 --> 00:19:29.175
like one continuous scene-

00:19:29.175 --> 00:19:30.008
- Oh, I see.

00:19:30.008 --> 00:19:31.286
- Camera changes or the scene changes.

00:19:31.286 --> 00:19:32.887
- So, you know, when in live action

00:19:32.887 --> 00:19:35.233
when you film something
and then the camera cuts,

00:19:35.233 --> 00:19:39.097
obviously in animation you
don't have an entire scene

00:19:39.097 --> 00:19:41.139
that you cut out of that's preplanned.

00:19:41.139 --> 00:19:42.378
- Right, right, that makes sense.

00:19:42.378 --> 00:19:45.138
- And so one scene can be one second

00:19:45.138 --> 00:19:46.832
or 10 second, 30 second.

00:19:46.832 --> 00:19:47.665
It depends.

00:19:47.665 --> 00:19:51.919
And if there is a lot of
seconds, it's more work.

00:19:51.919 --> 00:19:52.859
- Right, right, naturally.

00:19:52.859 --> 00:19:55.442
- And so let's take an example.

00:19:56.608 --> 00:19:58.228
Okay, we choose this sequence.

00:19:58.228 --> 00:19:59.313
It's 10 cuts.

00:19:59.313 --> 00:20:03.482
And so I go to the meeting,
the meeting with the director,

00:20:03.482 --> 00:20:06.649
and he going to explain me how he see,

00:20:08.328 --> 00:20:10.911
how he want those 10 cuts made.

00:20:12.222 --> 00:20:13.239
And he speak to me,

00:20:13.239 --> 00:20:17.434
"Yeah, for this cut, I would
like a rough animation,

00:20:17.434 --> 00:20:20.757
"a more action, like a form of West coast

00:20:20.757 --> 00:20:22.590
"or we'll say sakuga."

00:20:23.428 --> 00:20:25.461
(everyone laughs)

00:20:25.461 --> 00:20:26.419
- Sakuga.

00:20:26.419 --> 00:20:28.399
- Sakuga is like the Western slang word

00:20:28.399 --> 00:20:29.232
for like good animation.

00:20:29.232 --> 00:20:33.389
- Yeah, so the word
sakuga in Western world is

00:20:33.389 --> 00:20:37.150
completely different to what
sakuga in Japanese actually is.

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:37.983
- Yeah.

00:20:37.983 --> 00:20:40.027
- So in the anime community,

00:20:40.027 --> 00:20:42.732
we refer to sakuga as
good animation, right?

00:20:42.732 --> 00:20:44.434
Like lots of frames,
lots of flashy animation.

00:20:44.434 --> 00:20:47.022
But in Japanese sakuga just means frames.

00:20:47.022 --> 00:20:48.305
- Okay, okay, okay.

00:20:48.305 --> 00:20:52.305
- You look at the kanji
saku make, that picture.

00:20:53.617 --> 00:20:56.567
- So it's kind of become
like a buzz word here.

00:20:56.567 --> 00:20:57.549
- Yeah, that makes sense.

00:20:57.549 --> 00:21:00.373
- Like, if you said anime in Japan.

00:21:00.373 --> 00:21:02.606
Anime is animation.

00:21:02.606 --> 00:21:05.894
But if you say anime in West, it's-

00:21:05.894 --> 00:21:07.055
- Only Japanese anime.

00:21:07.055 --> 00:21:08.006
- It's only Japanese anime.

00:21:08.006 --> 00:21:10.152
- So in Japan, "Monsters
Inc" is technically an anime.

00:21:10.152 --> 00:21:10.985
