animators, a lot of veterans,

00:32:11.107 --> 00:32:14.220
and the staff was very nice to me.

00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:15.637
It was at Mitaka.

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:19.780
- Right.

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:21.754
- Yeah, the stuff was very nice.

00:32:21.754 --> 00:32:24.945
Wondering, "Why do you go to Japan?"

00:32:24.945 --> 00:32:27.256
(everyone laughs)

00:32:27.256 --> 00:32:28.089
No.

00:32:28.089 --> 00:32:30.172
And yeah, I wanted to go.

00:32:31.422 --> 00:32:33.422
I want to work in Japan.

00:32:34.537 --> 00:32:36.439
- Yeah, you want to be
an animator for anime.

00:32:36.439 --> 00:32:37.828
Man, I feel bad for our intern now.

00:32:37.828 --> 00:32:38.786
(everyone laughs)

00:32:38.786 --> 00:32:40.694
Like, we're sorry, Ashley.

00:32:40.694 --> 00:32:42.046
Like, hearing this story, you know.

00:32:42.046 --> 00:32:44.710
(Garnt laughs)

00:32:44.710 --> 00:32:48.449
- And I had met a senpai, a French senpai

00:32:48.449 --> 00:32:52.071
who by the name of Christophe Ferreira.

00:32:52.071 --> 00:32:54.282
- The most French name I've
ever heard. (Garnt laughs)

00:32:54.282 --> 00:32:58.615
- And he said to me, take
time and a work very late.

00:32:59.685 --> 00:33:01.400
And when you work very late,

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:04.417
there are less animators
but the boss is not here.

00:33:04.417 --> 00:33:06.012
And so anyway, he says,

00:33:06.012 --> 00:33:08.929
they are more free to speak to you.

00:33:10.550 --> 00:33:11.947
- More socializing going on.

00:33:11.947 --> 00:33:13.725
- Yeah, more socializing at night.

00:33:13.725 --> 00:33:16.308
Oh, you work harder, very late.

00:33:17.610 --> 00:33:19.258
What are you doing?

00:33:19.258 --> 00:33:21.215
- I feel like that must be
your bonding experience, right?

00:33:21.215 --> 00:33:22.939
'Cause you're staying up late together.

00:33:22.939 --> 00:33:24.522
- Exactly.

00:33:24.522 --> 00:33:28.683
When most of my good animators friends was

00:33:28.683 --> 00:33:31.100
because yes, we work very at,

00:33:33.051 --> 00:33:36.758
at eight o'clock in the
morning because we work hard.

00:33:36.758 --> 00:33:38.283
We didn't slept.

00:33:38.283 --> 00:33:40.394
"And your my brother now."

00:33:40.394 --> 00:33:42.746
(everyone laughs)

00:33:42.746 --> 00:33:43.579
- My man!

00:33:44.898 --> 00:33:47.844
- Damn so, you literally just
sent an email to a company

00:33:47.844 --> 00:33:51.047
and then you flew to Japan and wow.

00:33:51.047 --> 00:33:53.183
- And then he worked on fuckin' "Lupin."

00:33:53.183 --> 00:33:54.728
- Literally within a month.

00:33:54.728 --> 00:33:56.989
So, what timeframe was it from you sending

00:33:56.989 --> 00:33:58.138
that email to the company

00:33:58.138 --> 00:34:01.194
to you being in Japan
working on "Lupin the Third?"

00:34:01.194 --> 00:34:02.027
- Yeah.

00:34:02.027 --> 00:34:04.277
So I sent email in January.

00:34:05.835 --> 00:34:07.221
They said, "okay."

00:34:07.221 --> 00:34:09.101
And so I went in June.

00:34:09.101 --> 00:34:10.059
- Wow.

00:34:10.059 --> 00:34:11.017
- Like, six months.

00:34:11.017 --> 00:34:12.522
- Six months.

00:34:12.522 --> 00:34:13.980
- That's crazy.

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:16.730
- But yes, but I was pretty lucky

00:34:17.583 --> 00:34:19.132
because the director said yes

00:34:19.132 --> 00:34:23.359
and now the director is no more
working at Telecom Animation

00:34:23.359 --> 00:34:26.245
because he's now in Thai.

00:34:26.245 --> 00:34:28.351
And yeah, so it was cool.

00:34:28.351 --> 00:34:30.802
And so this was my first experience

00:34:30.802 --> 00:34:33.614
in the Japanese animation industry.

00:34:33.614 --> 00:34:35.375
It was 10 years ago.

00:34:35.375 --> 00:34:39.316
And so I went to another animation school.

00:34:39.316 --> 00:34:41.238
And to this animation school,

00:34:41.238 --> 00:34:45.803
I had the occasion to do a
summer school at Gobelins.

00:34:45.803 --> 00:34:47.728
L'Ecole de L'Image.

00:34:47.728 --> 00:34:50.444
It's a French famous school for animation.

00:34:50.444 --> 00:34:52.777
And I had met Thomas Romain.

00:34:55.317 --> 00:34:56.563
He's very famous

00:34:56.563 --> 00:35:01.563
because he's maybe the top
of French animators in Japan.

00:35:01.983 --> 00:35:03.893
And also because he's very famous,

00:35:03.893 --> 00:35:06.310
he redrew his kids' drawings.

00:35:07.793 --> 00:35:09.273
- [Garnt] Oh, I might've-

00:35:09.273 --> 00:35:10.390
- [Connor] That sounds familiar.

00:35:10.390 --> 00:35:11.513
- That sounds very familiar.

00:35:11.513 --> 00:35:14.578
That sounds like he blew up
on the Reddit or something.

00:35:14.578 --> 00:35:16.600
- Yeah, so his kids drew,

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:19.117
and he picked a picture of his kids

00:35:19.117 --> 00:35:21.023
and redrew it as a professional.

00:35:21.023 --> 00:35:23.278
- Yeah, that sounds very familiar.

00:35:23.278 --> 00:35:24.861
Yeah.

00:35:24.861 --> 00:35:28.216
- I met him at this summer school.

00:35:28.216 --> 00:35:30.383
And I talk a bit with him,

00:35:32.031 --> 00:35:35.448
and he gave me his cards, business cards.

00:35:36.556 --> 00:35:39.443
And connection is very important.

00:35:39.443 --> 00:35:40.526
- Yeah.

00:35:40.526 --> 00:35:43.840
- Like I said, this summer
school, I could do it

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:47.388
because in fact a director
of Telecom Animation

00:35:47.388 --> 00:35:50.182
was doing summer school at Gobelins,

00:35:50.182 --> 00:35:52.088
but he needed a translator.

00:35:52.088 --> 00:35:53.329
- Oh.

00:35:53.329 --> 00:35:54.457
- And he called me.

00:35:54.457 --> 00:35:56.443
He goes, "Oh, you're in
France, you can help me."

00:35:56.443 --> 00:35:57.525
- [Connor] Right, right, right.

00:35:57.525 --> 00:35:58.854
- And that's why,

00:35:58.854 --> 00:36:01.299
the first way to find a
contact is very difficult.

00:36:01.299 --> 00:36:04.944
But when you have one, he can
introduce you to someone else.

00:36:04.944 --> 00:36:06.327
- Yeah, the door opens.

00:36:06.327 --> 00:36:08.362
- But that's what it is with,
like, the anime industry is

00:36:08.362 --> 00:36:10.202
that, like, it's only so big.

00:36:10.202 --> 00:36:12.357
So once, you know, one person-

00:36:12.357 --> 00:36:15.152
- The hard part is getting in,

00:36:15.152 --> 00:36:17.681
but everyone I've met who is
working in the anime industry

00:36:17.681 --> 00:36:19.213
especially if they're a foreigner,

00:36:19.213 --> 00:36:22.466
they've just had like that
one lucky pull of, like,

00:36:22.466 --> 00:36:24.361
just like the one lucky in.

00:36:24.361 --> 00:36:25.470
And once they've got that in.

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:29.438
- As new animators the first
time it's difficult to enter

00:36:29.438 --> 00:36:30.617
but after it's to stay.

00:36:30.617 --> 00:36:33.070
(everyone laughs)

00:36:33.070 --> 00:36:36.726
But so, I sent a mailer to Thomas Romain.

00:36:36.726 --> 00:36:41.294
And Thomas Romain was working
at this time at Satelight.

00:36:41.294 --> 00:36:45.503
So, they're very famous
for the series "Macross."

00:36:45.503 --> 00:36:46.807
- Another animation studio, I assume?

00:36:46.807 --> 00:36:47.765
- Yeah.

00:36:47.765 --> 00:36:51.664
- They made "Log Horizon"
as well, season...

00:36:51.664 --> 00:36:52.622
One of the seasons.

00:36:52.622 --> 00:36:53.705
- Season two?

00:36:54.955 --> 00:36:59.372
- And so I could manage to
do a four month internship

00:37:01.237 --> 00:37:02.320
at Satelight.

00:37:03.648 --> 00:37:06.898
But it was very different from Telecom.

00:37:08.657 --> 00:37:10.740
It was a bit more colder,

00:37:11.669 --> 00:37:13.002
a bit more rush.

00:37:14.561 --> 00:37:16.115
"Oh, hurry up, hurry up."

00:37:16.115 --> 00:37:20.412
At Telecom the animators were coming at 10

00:37:20.412 --> 00:37:22.716
and leaving at night or like at 10 or so.

00:37:22.716 --> 00:37:27.497
But at Satelight, everybody
was coming in the afternoon,

00:37:27.497 --> 00:37:30.830
two or four, and was working at 6:00 AM.

00:37:32.173 --> 00:37:33.513
- Oh my God.

00:37:33.513 --> 00:37:34.596
Jesus.

00:37:34.596 --> 00:37:36.250
- Definitely a lot more like hardcore.

00:37:36.250 --> 00:37:37.589
I guess, like, what I
want to ask is, like,

00:37:37.589 --> 00:37:39.320
kind of going back to
"Lupin the Third" thing.

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:40.851
Like, I assume you, like,

00:37:40.851 --> 00:37:44.047
watched the TV show
after it came out, right?

00:37:44.047 --> 00:37:44.880
- Yeah.

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:46.231
- I've always wanted to
know, like, as an animator,

00:37:46.231 --> 00:37:48.176
like, how does it feel to see something

00:37:48.176 --> 00:37:50.657
that you drew be animated on TV?

00:37:50.657 --> 00:37:52.164
'Cause like, I can't
imagine what that's like.

00:37:52.164 --> 00:37:55.141
- So sometimes you, "Oh, shit I screw up."

00:37:55.141 --> 00:37:58.617
(everyone laughs)

00:37:58.617 --> 00:38:00.617
I find, like, there are,

00:38:01.990 --> 00:38:04.573
I managed to do something good.

00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:09.260
When some few weeks
passed, look at it again.

00:38:10.116 --> 00:38:11.795
(Garnt laughs)

00:38:11.795 --> 00:38:13.551
You're not very satisfied.

00:38:13.551 --> 00:38:16.341
- That's how you know you've grown, right?

00:38:16.341 --> 00:38:18.530
You hate everything you've made.

00:38:18.530 --> 00:38:21.664
- The way for to grow in
drawings is very difficult

00:38:21.664 --> 00:38:23.649
because it's not like that,

00:38:23.649 --> 00:38:25.066
it's like stairs.

00:38:26.049 --> 00:38:27.548
- Right, right, right.

00:38:27.548 --> 00:38:31.246
- And sometimes the days
are long flat momentum.

00:38:31.246 --> 00:38:32.829
You don't progress.

00:38:33.781 --> 00:38:35.619
And yeah, you,

00:38:35.619 --> 00:38:38.363
it's a, yeah, you fight against you,

00:38:38.363 --> 00:38:42.895
between prov and sometimes
you can depress sometimes.

00:38:42.895 --> 00:38:44.372
- But I'm sure there's been moments

00:38:44.372 --> 00:38:47.229
where, you know, there was like
a cut that was really good.

00:38:47.229 --> 00:38:48.869
And you're just like, "Yeah, I did that."

00:38:48.869 --> 00:38:49.792
- Yeah, there is.

00:38:49.792 --> 00:38:50.625
There is moment.

00:38:50.625 --> 00:38:51.952
And when you have this moment,

00:38:51.952 --> 00:38:54.537
it's the moment, "oh
man, animation is cool."

00:38:54.537 --> 00:38:56.612
(everyone laughs)

00:38:56.612 --> 00:38:57.923
- I think it's the same for us

00:38:57.923 --> 00:38:59.090
when we make like a YouTube video.

00:38:59.090 --> 00:39:01.701
Sometimes we just feel like
we've stagnated in content.

00:39:01.701 --> 00:39:03.367
And sometimes-
- How do I beat this?

00:39:03.367 --> 00:39:05.327
- Sometimes we put out that
one banger video and like,

00:39:05.327 --> 00:39:07.464
"Yeah, this is why I do YouTube."

00:39:07.464 --> 00:39:09.642
And then you hate your job
for the rest of the time.

00:39:09.642 --> 00:39:12.414
- And also when you can talk
animation with your senpai

00:39:12.414 --> 00:39:14.252
or your coworkers is very good.

00:39:14.252 --> 00:39:17.074
And share the things you like.

00:39:17.074 --> 00:39:20.838
The way of thinking of
animation is the same as me.

00:39:20.838 --> 00:39:22.806
Yeah, it's cool.

00:39:22.806 --> 00:39:25.280
And so talking about
animation with your senpai,

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:29.345
and you learn a lot of
history of a famous show.

00:39:29.345 --> 00:39:33.399
At this point when they done
this episode, there was broken.

00:39:33.399 --> 00:39:35.982
But they managed to succeed it.

00:39:38.035 --> 00:39:40.148
- I've gotta ask what's the first show

00:39:40.148 --> 00:39:42.387
that you were given that you were, like,

00:39:42.387 --> 00:39:44.028
really, really excited for?

00:39:44.028 --> 00:39:47.341
Like, when you realize, "Oh,
I'm working on this show?

00:39:47.341 --> 00:39:48.174
"Really?

00:39:48.174 --> 00:39:49.007
"This show?"

00:39:49.007 --> 00:39:49.840
What was the first time?

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:50.840
- "Dragon Ball Super Broly."

00:39:50.840 --> 00:39:52.464
- "Dragon Ball," of course!

00:39:52.464 --> 00:39:53.594
Of course, I bet it was.

00:39:53.594 --> 00:39:56.007
- When I was little, I was
watching "Dragon Ball."

00:39:56.007 --> 00:39:58.757
Goku, I was imitating kamehameha.

00:40:00.380 --> 00:40:02.213
And so I had, so yeah.

00:40:04.485 --> 00:40:07.748
"Dragon Ball," I had
the chance to work on it

00:40:07.748 --> 00:40:09.863
because my senpai,

00:40:09.863 --> 00:40:12.200
I was at Yapiko at this moment.

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:14.867
And I had start as key animator.

00:40:15.873 --> 00:40:16.831
- [Connor] Wow.

00:40:16.831 --> 00:40:21.295
- And yeah, so we skip a
lot of years to Graphinica

00:40:21.295 --> 00:40:23.359
where I was in-betweener.

00:40:23.359 --> 00:40:25.359
So when I was at Yapiko,

00:40:26.954 --> 00:40:31.480
my senpai wanted to work on
"Dragon Ball Super," the series.

00:40:31.480 --> 00:40:33.813
But so we had another senpai

00:40:35.544 --> 00:40:38.377
who had worked on "Restriction F,"

00:40:39.536 --> 00:40:41.804
previous movie of "Dragon Ball Super."

00:40:41.804 --> 00:40:43.110
- Okay, "Resurrection F."

00:40:43.110 --> 00:40:45.443
- And his name is Shimizu...

00:40:47.205 --> 00:40:48.737
Oh, I forgot.

00:40:48.737 --> 00:40:49.570
- Shimizusan.

00:40:49.570 --> 00:40:52.244
(everyone laughs)

00:40:52.244 --> 00:40:53.852
- And Hiroshi Shimizu.

00:40:53.852 --> 00:40:56.496
He used to do character for
days I know for manga books.

00:40:56.496 --> 00:40:57.507
- Oh shit.

00:40:57.507 --> 00:40:58.340
Okay.

00:40:58.340 --> 00:41:02.403
- And he, so he introduced us to Toriya.

00:41:02.403 --> 00:41:04.666
"Hey, those French people
want to work on Dragon Ball."

00:41:04.666 --> 00:41:06.528
(everyone laughs)

00:41:06.528 --> 00:41:08.611
And so Toriya met us and,

00:41:09.736 --> 00:41:12.099
"Okay, so we have this episode.

00:41:12.099 --> 00:41:15.682
"You have a Vegeta and
a Goku fight these,"

00:41:16.692 --> 00:41:19.066
I don't remember the name of the man.

00:41:19.066 --> 00:41:20.096
He wears white.

00:41:20.096 --> 00:41:20.929
- So someone's just like,

00:41:20.929 --> 00:41:22.003
"These guys want to work on Dragon Ball."

00:41:22.003 --> 00:41:25.503
And Toriya's literally like, "Yeah, sure."

00:41:26.525 --> 00:41:28.342
- We had a recommendation.

00:41:28.342 --> 00:41:29.752
Senpai, okay.

00:41:29.752 --> 00:41:34.146
So at first, do the series
and if the series was good,

00:41:34.146 --> 00:41:36.159
okay, maybe you can work on-

00:41:36.159 --> 00:41:37.117
- [Connor] Do more, right.

00:41:37.117 --> 00:41:37.950
- On the movie.

00:41:37.950 --> 00:41:39.886
And the movie wasn't
announced at this moment.

00:41:39.886 --> 00:41:40.969
- Right.

00:41:40.969 --> 00:41:42.067
- And so, okay.

00:41:42.067 --> 00:41:44.234
Let's work on the episode.

00:41:45.072 --> 00:41:49.525
But when they sent us the
storyboard and the schedule,

00:41:49.525 --> 00:41:50.901
there was not a good match

00:41:50.901 --> 00:41:53.454
because it was the end of December.

00:41:53.454 --> 00:41:56.581
And most of the French
people was going back to-

00:41:56.581 --> 00:41:57.414
- Christmas.

00:41:57.414 --> 00:41:58.544
- Christmas, and so,

00:41:58.544 --> 00:42:02.249
"Sorry, can you give us another storyboard

00:42:02.249 --> 00:42:04.298
"to finish the episode?"

00:42:04.298 --> 00:42:05.131
Okay.

00:42:06.131 --> 00:42:08.577
"Well, by the way, how about the movie?"

00:42:08.577 --> 00:42:10.626
(everyone laughs)

00:42:10.626 --> 00:42:11.459
Sure.

00:42:12.582 --> 00:42:13.542
- Just casually throw that-

00:42:13.542 --> 00:42:15.443
- So they tested you out on the movie?

00:42:15.443 --> 00:42:16.276
- Yeah.

00:42:16.276 --> 00:42:17.247
- Oh my God.

00:42:17.247 --> 00:42:18.477
- What a trial run.

00:42:18.477 --> 00:42:19.685
Imagine that.

00:42:19.685 --> 00:42:24.685
- And it was the first time
I was doing genga on a movie.

00:42:24.894 --> 00:42:27.581
So, I had some pressure

00:42:27.581 --> 00:42:28.988
- Some pressure?

00:42:28.988 --> 00:42:29.821
- Yeah.

00:42:29.821 --> 00:42:34.562
Pressure because you know that
"Dragon Ball" fans can be-

00:42:34.562 --> 00:42:36.834
(everyone laughs)

00:42:36.834 --> 00:42:38.200
- We've talked about it before.

00:42:38.200 --> 00:42:40.168
We've talked about
"Dragon Ball" fans before.

00:42:40.168 --> 00:42:42.327
- Are "Dragon Ball" fans the same in Japan

00:42:42.327 --> 00:42:43.219
like they are in the West?

00:42:43.219 --> 00:42:44.487
Or were you talking about
the West specifically?

00:42:44.487 --> 00:42:45.570
- I don't know.

00:42:45.570 --> 00:42:47.950
Some people can say bad things.

00:42:47.950 --> 00:42:49.935
(everyone laughs)

00:42:49.935 --> 00:42:51.242
- It's different when an animator

00:42:51.242 --> 00:42:52.877
who worked on the movie is saying it.

00:42:52.877 --> 00:42:53.960
- I know right.

00:42:53.960 --> 00:42:57.361
- So I had the pressure, but I was happy.

00:42:57.361 --> 00:42:59.081
Man, I'm working on "Dragon Ball."

00:42:59.081 --> 00:43:01.451
And yes, it's cool.

00:43:01.451 --> 00:43:04.568
And so when I was doing my
drawings on "Dragon Ball,"

00:43:04.568 --> 00:43:07.446
I would (sings)

00:43:07.446 --> 00:43:08.794
(everyone laughs)

00:43:08.794 --> 00:43:10.166
I was sitting at this table,

00:43:10.166 --> 00:43:11.324
but I was listening "Dragon Ball"

00:43:11.324 --> 00:43:12.599
and doing "Dragon Ball" stuff.

00:43:12.599 --> 00:43:13.779
- Hyping yourself up.

00:43:13.779 --> 00:43:16.604
- And I have my buddy Midia Showi

00:43:16.604 --> 00:43:21.046
who was on the schedule and
doing our "Dragon Ball."

00:43:21.046 --> 00:43:21.879
- Oh my God.

00:43:21.879 --> 00:43:23.100
- So, you went from being an intern

00:43:23.100 --> 00:43:24.465
to working on "Lupin"

00:43:24.465 --> 00:43:26.025
and then being an in-betweener

00:43:26.025 --> 00:43:27.970
to just going straight into
the "Dragon Ball Super-"

00:43:27.970 --> 00:43:28.803
- No, no, no.

00:43:28.803 --> 00:43:30.386
Of course not.

00:43:30.386 --> 00:43:33.365
So, I have done my internship
at Telecom Animation Film

00:43:33.365 --> 00:43:34.303
and Satelight.

00:43:34.303 --> 00:43:36.745
So I have done two internships.

00:43:36.745 --> 00:43:39.241
Six months, if you put it-

00:43:39.241 --> 00:43:40.980
- Put it all together.

00:43:40.980 --> 00:43:44.385
- And when I finished my school, I said,

00:43:44.385 --> 00:43:46.111
okay, I have done two internship.

00:43:46.111 --> 00:43:49.721
I know it's hard that
Telicom was a good mood,

00:43:49.721 --> 00:43:53.145
at Satelight it was more cold
and you have to more hurry

00:43:53.145 --> 00:43:55.455
and you are paid also by frame.

00:43:55.455 --> 00:43:56.892
So some money-

00:43:56.892 --> 00:43:57.725
- Oh shit.

00:43:57.725 --> 00:44:00.006
- Yeah, it's per frame
here rather than per day.

00:44:00.006 --> 00:44:03.695
- Am I allowed to ask you
what that might be per frame?

00:44:03.695 --> 00:44:05.884
- Well, when you're in-between,

00:44:05.884 --> 00:44:08.322
you are paid like two euros per frame.

00:44:08.322 --> 00:44:09.915
- Two euros per frame?

00:44:09.915 --> 00:44:10.748
- Yeah.

00:44:10.748 --> 00:44:11.748
- Holy shit.

00:44:12.722 --> 00:44:14.456
- What is that in USD?

00:44:14.456 --> 00:44:17.090
- So you would draw a
frame, go to Family Mart,

00:44:17.090 --> 00:44:18.679
get a sandwich and you just-

00:44:18.679 --> 00:44:19.768
- Yes, I use it.

00:44:19.768 --> 00:44:22.685
- It's like three or four bucks US.

00:44:23.948 --> 00:44:26.406
- Before tax, right?

00:44:26.406 --> 00:44:27.364
- Yeah.

00:44:27.364 --> 00:44:29.540
- I assume you wouldn't even make enough

00:44:29.540 --> 00:44:31.839
to go for the tax cut.

00:44:31.839 --> 00:44:34.422
- So when you take a drink you,

00:44:35.433 --> 00:44:38.283
"Okay, can I buy a couple maybe?

00:44:38.283 --> 00:44:40.209
"Oh, I have to do more frames."

00:44:40.209 --> 00:44:43.541
- So, you're making a conscious choice.

00:44:43.541 --> 00:44:46.654
I need to draw more so
that I can eat and drink.

00:44:46.654 --> 00:44:49.377
- Okay, so how long would
you say it takes you

00:44:49.377 --> 00:44:50.936
to do a frame?

00:44:50.936 --> 00:44:51.769
- It depends.

00:44:51.769 --> 00:44:52.602
- Depends?

00:44:52.602 --> 00:44:54.282
- The frame can be just five minutes.

00:44:54.282 --> 00:44:55.662
- Right.

00:44:55.662 --> 00:44:58.206
- Some frame can take
you two, three hours.

00:44:58.206 --> 00:44:59.039
- Oh my.

00:45:00.414 --> 00:45:01.393
That's brutal.

00:45:01.393 --> 00:45:03.404
- I mean, this has been a hot button topic

00:45:03.404 --> 00:45:04.266
in the industry for a while.

00:45:04.266 --> 00:45:05.538
- I think a lot of people do talk

00:45:05.538 --> 00:45:07.113
about that animators get paid the least.

00:45:07.113 --> 00:45:09.556
- Just hearing it from an
actual animator, just, you know.

00:45:09.556 --> 00:45:14.099
- And also because nowadays
the anime getting more details

00:45:14.099 --> 00:45:15.617
and more, more lines.

00:45:15.617 --> 00:45:16.667
- Has the price gone up?

00:45:16.667 --> 00:45:17.834
- More sakuga.

00:45:19.250 --> 00:45:23.083
- It was 50 years ago
when you draw one frame,

00:45:24.236 --> 00:45:26.069
you can eat one ramen.

00:45:27.809 --> 00:45:28.642
- [Connor] Right.

00:45:28.642 --> 00:45:31.256
- But the rate of their life increase.

00:45:31.256 --> 00:45:32.337
- Inflation.

00:45:32.337 --> 00:45:34.221
- Yeah, the price.

00:45:34.221 --> 00:45:35.863
- Damn you inflation.

00:45:35.863 --> 00:45:37.929
- So now if you want to eat a ramen,

00:45:37.929 --> 00:45:40.662
you have at least to do five or six frame.

00:45:40.662 --> 00:45:41.620
- Oh my.

00:45:41.620 --> 00:45:43.067
- So, if you don't mind me asking,

00:45:43.067 --> 00:45:44.693
when you did your internship initially,

00:45:44.693 --> 00:45:46.329
how much were you getting paid for that?

00:45:46.329 --> 00:45:47.658
And were you living off just that?

00:45:47.658 --> 00:45:50.047
- At the first internship I wasn't paid.

00:45:50.047 --> 00:45:50.880
- Oh, wow.

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:52.801
- Yeah, but the second
internship I was paid.

00:45:52.801 --> 00:45:54.454
- So, it really was like a passion thing.

00:45:54.454 --> 00:45:56.188
- Yes, it was a passion thing then.

00:45:56.188 --> 00:46:00.177
I was knowing that my
internship you are paid or not,

00:46:00.177 --> 00:46:04.622
but I was paid by frame for
my internship at Satelight.

00:46:04.622 --> 00:46:07.782
And so I knew, okay, it's really hard.

00:46:07.782 --> 00:46:09.786
So I tried to do my best.

00:46:09.786 --> 00:46:11.786
But yeah, my best record

00:46:13.461 --> 00:46:16.476
when I was in internship was just 150.

00:46:16.476 --> 00:46:17.502
- 150 frames?

00:46:17.502 --> 00:46:18.335
- Yeah.

00:46:18.335 --> 00:46:19.759
- So like $300?

00:46:19.759 --> 00:46:20.592
- Yeah.

00:46:20.592 --> 00:46:21.675
- Oh my God.

00:46:21.675 --> 00:46:23.924
For a month of work.

00:46:23.924 --> 00:46:26.539
And a specialized job as well.

00:46:26.539 --> 00:46:27.942
- So this wasn't my internship.

00:46:27.942 --> 00:46:29.559
And so it was on paper.

00:46:29.559 --> 00:46:31.599
- So wait, the guy who was
probably cleaning the toilets

00:46:31.599 --> 00:46:33.572
at the studio was probably
getting paid more than you.

00:46:33.572 --> 00:46:35.989
(Ken laughs)

00:46:36.888 --> 00:46:38.666
- Honestly, it's probably true.

00:46:38.666 --> 00:46:40.469
- Actually if you work at-

00:46:40.469 --> 00:46:41.997
(everyone laughs)

00:46:41.997 --> 00:46:44.777
- Just work in your local Family Mart.

00:46:44.777 --> 00:46:45.967
- I just want to put it into perspective.

00:46:45.967 --> 00:46:49.090
Not trying to diminish any
jobs, but like, I mean,

00:46:49.090 --> 00:46:51.461
you know, cleaning a toilet
is not a specialized skill.

00:46:51.461 --> 00:46:53.172
- Literally working at McDonald's.

00:46:53.172 --> 00:46:55.194
- Especially considering
how art school is, like,

00:46:55.194 --> 00:46:57.018
one of the most expensive schools.

00:46:57.018 --> 00:46:58.965
- Which is why I'm not trying
to belittle any of the jobs,

00:46:58.965 --> 00:46:59.798
we need those jobs.

00:46:59.798 --> 00:47:00.631
But like, yeah.

00:47:00.631 --> 00:47:03.386
The fact that it is a specialized job.

00:47:03.386 --> 00:47:04.886
Your best was 300?

00:47:05.934 --> 00:47:07.142
Bruh!

00:47:07.142 --> 00:47:08.350
- It was my internship.

00:47:08.350 --> 00:47:10.148
So, it was, I had an-

00:47:10.148 --> 00:47:11.188
- Still for internship.

00:47:11.188 --> 00:47:13.534
That's not enough to live, right?

00:47:13.534 --> 00:47:16.861
- But like I said, my mother's Japanese,

00:47:16.861 --> 00:47:19.395
I had a family in Japan in Tokyo.

00:47:19.395 --> 00:47:20.228
So, yes.

00:47:21.753 --> 00:47:23.240
It was cool because my family.

00:47:23.240 --> 00:47:26.258
I was living in the
house of my grandmother.

00:47:26.258 --> 00:47:27.091
So it was a-

00:47:27.091 --> 00:47:28.299
- Oh, okay.

00:47:28.299 --> 00:47:29.889
- You didn't have to pay
for, like, rent or anything.

00:47:29.889 --> 00:47:32.348
- Yes because I had luck.

00:47:32.348 --> 00:47:35.253
But, so I was thinking,
man, for foreign people

00:47:35.253 --> 00:47:37.351
if you don't have family.

00:47:37.351 --> 00:47:39.111
- Yeah, I was about to say, like-

00:47:39.111 --> 00:47:41.925
- Did you have friends who
didn't have family here?

00:47:41.925 --> 00:47:42.758
- Yeah.

00:47:46.228 --> 00:47:48.481
They could negotiate better than me.

00:47:48.481 --> 00:47:49.314
- Right, right, right.

00:47:49.314 --> 00:47:51.494
- So that's why you'll have to,

00:47:51.494 --> 00:47:55.426
now the good advice is you have to look

00:47:55.426 --> 00:48:00.088
at the good studio to
take a lot of information

00:48:00.088 --> 00:48:01.421
of each schedule.

00:48:01.421 --> 00:48:02.401
How do they pay?

00:48:02.401 --> 00:48:03.234
Et cetera.

00:48:04.359 --> 00:48:06.686
Yeah, it was just intuition.

00:48:06.686 --> 00:48:09.268
So I cannot take example of Satelight,

00:48:09.268 --> 00:48:11.279
but I more example on Graphinica,

00:48:11.279 --> 00:48:14.357
the studio I was doing in-betweens

00:48:14.357 --> 00:48:15.578
but digital in-betweens.

00:48:15.578 --> 00:48:16.909
- Right, right, right.

00:48:16.909 --> 00:48:19.203
- Digital I was more quicker.

00:48:19.203 --> 00:48:20.036
- Okay.

00:48:20.036 --> 00:48:21.390
- But was the pay the same?

00:48:21.390 --> 00:48:23.790
- The pay was the same.

00:48:23.790 --> 00:48:27.947
The pay was the same, but we had a kotaku.

00:48:27.947 --> 00:48:32.284
So, you have a little
fixer amount of salary,

00:48:32.284 --> 00:48:34.784
and it was at least 400 euros.

00:48:38.029 --> 00:48:38.862
- A month?

00:48:38.862 --> 00:48:39.820
- A month.

00:48:39.820 --> 00:48:43.487
And they ask you to do
300 frames per month.

00:48:45.675 --> 00:48:48.175
It was at least do 300 frames.

00:48:49.164 --> 00:48:50.528
- At least 300 frames a month?

00:48:50.528 --> 00:48:51.361
- Yes.

00:48:51.361 --> 00:48:55.277
And so when you success to do 300 frames,

00:48:55.277 --> 00:48:57.516
your sort of 300 frames two euros,

00:48:57.516 --> 00:49:00.433
you have 600 plus the fixed salary.

00:49:01.328 --> 00:49:02.970
- So, it's about 1,000 euros.

00:49:02.970 --> 00:49:03.803
- 1,000 euros.

00:49:03.803 --> 00:49:05.870
- Still for a specialist
job, that's still quite low.

00:49:05.870 --> 00:49:06.712
- Yeah, it's still very low compared

00:49:06.712 --> 00:49:07.560
to other specialist jobs.

00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:12.393
- But my best record when I
was doing in-betweens was 600.

00:49:16.471 --> 00:49:17.702
- 600 frames in a month?

00:49:17.702 --> 00:49:19.298
- Yeah, 600 frames a month.

00:49:19.298 --> 00:49:20.131
So, 600...

00:49:22.408 --> 00:49:23.658
- 1,200 euros.

00:49:24.677 --> 00:49:28.402
- So, you can at least have 1,500 euros.

00:49:28.402 --> 00:49:30.522
- For an internship that's
probably what $1,500.

00:49:30.522 --> 00:49:32.245
That's probably not bad for an internship.

00:49:32.245 --> 00:49:33.607
- This wasn't an internship.

00:49:33.607 --> 00:49:34.563
- A real job?

00:49:34.563 --> 00:49:35.396
Oh, okay.

00:49:35.396 --> 00:49:36.737
For a real job that kinda sucks.

00:49:36.737 --> 00:49:37.737
- Okay, wow.

00:49:38.695 --> 00:49:40.302
- Meilyne's face over there.

00:49:40.302 --> 00:49:41.874
- Meilyne's like sweating right now.

00:49:41.874 --> 00:49:43.238
(everyone laughs)

00:49:43.238 --> 00:49:46.071
- So yes, so most of my coworkers,

00:49:47.793 --> 00:49:52.132
"I'm working, I'm living
at my parent's house.

00:49:52.132 --> 00:49:54.799
"I'm living with a share house."

00:49:56.810 --> 00:49:59.488
So, you have to pay
attention about your money

00:49:59.488 --> 00:50:00.388
and how you spend it.

00:50:00.388 --> 00:50:01.906
- It's not a glamorous lifestyle.

00:50:01.906 --> 00:50:02.739
- No.

00:50:02.739 --> 00:50:04.142
You don't live, you survive.

00:50:04.142 --> 00:50:04.975
- Right.

00:50:06.600 --> 00:50:08.063
I think a lot of people sometimes, like,

00:50:08.063 --> 00:50:09.811
I remember I've had people come up to me

00:50:09.811 --> 00:50:11.221
'cause I'll be like, with, like,

00:50:11.221 --> 00:50:13.282
I remember at Anime
Expo I was with Trigger.

00:50:13.282 --> 00:50:14.731
And they were like, "Oh,
they want a job at Trigger."

00:50:14.731 --> 00:50:17.916
And I'm like, do you really want a job

00:50:17.916 --> 00:50:20.248
where, like, you really
have to work your ass off-

00:50:20.248 --> 00:50:22.854
- What are your work hours like
if you don't mind me asking?

00:50:22.854 --> 00:50:23.937
- At Graphinica?

00:50:23.937 --> 00:50:24.770
- Yeah.

00:50:24.770 --> 00:50:29.353
- So I start at 10, and it
depends about the deadlines.

00:50:31.272 --> 00:50:33.297
You have some time I arrived

00:50:33.297 --> 00:50:36.950
and there are a lot of
cuts, and my senpai,

00:50:36.950 --> 00:50:39.110
"Oh, you have to finish
this for this evening."

00:50:39.110 --> 00:50:39.943
- [Connor] Yeah.

00:50:39.943 --> 00:50:40.901
- At eight.

00:50:40.901 --> 00:50:43.237
Okay, so I don't eat lunch.

00:50:43.237 --> 00:50:44.485
(Garnt laughs)

00:50:44.485 --> 00:50:45.665
- They're very opposite of-

00:50:45.665 --> 00:50:47.333
- Literally just eight hours straight.

00:50:47.333 --> 00:50:50.854
- And sometimes I have,
"Oh, some good cuts.

00:50:50.854 --> 00:50:52.206
"I want to do this.

00:50:52.206 --> 00:50:53.266
"Please let me do this.

00:50:53.266 --> 00:50:56.027
Okay, I need it in three days.

00:50:56.027 --> 00:50:56.860
Oh, fuck.

00:50:56.860 --> 00:50:58.453
I won't sleep I guess.

00:50:58.453 --> 00:50:59.523
(everyone laughs)

00:50:59.523 --> 00:51:01.244
Because I wanted to, it was Pokemon, guys.

00:51:01.244 --> 00:51:04.354
So I wanted to work Pokemon and yeah.

00:51:04.354 --> 00:51:05.989
- You make an exception
'cause you're like,

00:51:05.989 --> 00:51:06.822
"I like Pokemon.

00:51:06.822 --> 00:51:09.010
"I'll ruin my life for Pokemon."

00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:10.177
- It was blue.

00:51:11.343 --> 00:51:13.903
It was the Pokemon generation on YouTube.

00:51:13.903 --> 00:51:14.861
- Oh, cool.

00:51:14.861 --> 00:51:15.694
Oh, that one.

00:51:15.694 --> 00:51:17.152
I know that one.

00:51:17.152 --> 00:51:18.931
- Yeah, and it was blue with the,

00:51:18.931 --> 00:51:20.527
in Japanese you say Kamex.

00:51:20.527 --> 00:51:21.906
I don't know in English.

00:51:21.906 --> 00:51:22.906
- Blastoise.

00:51:23.864 --> 00:51:25.741
- There's French names
as well, isn't there?

00:51:25.741 --> 00:51:27.370
French localized names.

00:51:27.370 --> 00:51:29.033
- French name is Tortank.

00:51:29.033 --> 00:51:29.874
(everyone laughs)

00:51:29.874 --> 00:51:30.707
- Tortank!

00:51:30.707 --> 00:51:33.200
That sounds way more
badass than Blastoise.

00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:34.232
- I learned about that.

00:51:34.232 --> 00:51:35.822
Apparently the French
localizations were, like,

00:51:35.822 --> 00:51:37.334
really well done for Pokemon.

00:51:37.334 --> 00:51:38.862
Apparently it was
really, really well done.

00:51:38.862 --> 00:51:41.243
And all the names were,
like, really well thought out

00:51:41.243 --> 00:51:42.076
and to do with, like,

00:51:42.076 --> 00:51:44.079
a mix of Japanese and French mythologies.

00:51:44.079 --> 00:51:45.760
- I mean, Tortanks sounds-

00:51:45.760 --> 00:51:47.695
- Well, that just sounds badass.

00:51:47.695 --> 00:51:50.358
- Tortoise but it's a tank.

00:51:50.358 --> 00:51:51.816
- That's awesome.

00:51:51.816 --> 00:51:53.547
- Wow, that is like the very opposite

00:51:53.547 --> 00:51:55.152
of European lifestyle where it's like,

00:51:55.152 --> 00:51:55.985
"Oh, it's five?

00:51:55.985 --> 00:51:57.040
"Oh, the building's on fire?

00:51:57.040 --> 00:51:58.373
"Well it's five o'clock, I'm going home."

00:51:58.373 --> 00:52:00.081
(everyone laughs)

00:52:00.081 --> 00:52:04.034
- Yeah, in-between you
have to be passionate.

00:52:04.034 --> 00:52:05.623
You have to love animation.

00:52:05.623 --> 00:52:06.831
- I've heard that a lot.

00:52:06.831 --> 00:52:09.378
- And also it depends what
kind of experience you want.

00:52:09.378 --> 00:52:14.225
If it's just, "Oh, I want to
work just once for this show."

00:52:14.225 --> 00:52:17.368
On "Naruto" or "Once Piece," I don't know.

00:52:17.368 --> 00:52:21.796
"I just want one, two
years experience in Japan."

00:52:21.796 --> 00:52:24.851
No, I wanted to have a career in Japan.

00:52:24.851 --> 00:52:26.834
- It depends what experience
you're looking for.

00:52:26.834 --> 00:52:28.266
- So you often find people
come into the companies like,

00:52:28.266 --> 00:52:30.122
"I only want to be here for
a year or two then go back."

00:52:30.122 --> 00:52:31.330
- Yeah.

00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:34.451
Sometimes some people have come in Japan

00:52:34.451 --> 00:52:36.654
and they had some homesick

00:52:36.654 --> 00:52:39.384
because it was the first
time they came in Japan

00:52:39.384 --> 00:52:42.268
and the food didn't go well

00:52:42.268 --> 00:52:45.018
and they couldn't speak Japanese.

00:52:46.119 --> 00:52:48.702
So yeah, and English was so-so.

00:52:50.297 --> 00:52:52.957
So yeah, so it was difficult
to make some friends.

00:52:52.957 --> 00:52:53.790
And so, yes.

00:52:53.790 --> 00:52:57.290
You still hang around the same people, so.

00:52:59.433 --> 00:53:01.125
- It's just a completely
different lifestyles.

00:53:01.125 --> 00:53:03.356
- Did you know English
before you came to Japan?

00:53:03.356 --> 00:53:04.189
- Yeah.

00:53:04.189 --> 00:53:05.022
- Oh, okay.

00:53:05.022 --> 00:53:07.939
- I was learning English at school.

00:53:09.259 --> 00:53:11.540
- I gotta ask because
you grew up in France

00:53:11.540 --> 00:53:15.021
and moving you know
about, you know, how much,

00:53:15.021 --> 00:53:16.705
well you were telling us earlier

00:53:16.705 --> 00:53:17.757
about how much more we can get paid

00:53:17.757 --> 00:53:19.872
doing the same job being in France

00:53:19.872 --> 00:53:23.883
and also having, let's say,
less work hours as well.

00:53:23.883 --> 00:53:28.665
What made you decide to give
that up and move to Japan?

00:53:28.665 --> 00:53:30.331
And have like a-

00:53:30.331 --> 00:53:34.748
- Yeah, because when I tell
my story of my internship

00:53:35.813 --> 00:53:40.454
to my friends, "Ken, do you
really want to go to Japan?"

00:53:40.454 --> 00:53:41.755
(Garnt laughs)

00:53:41.755 --> 00:53:45.391
"It'd be a real issue because
for me I want to have a life."

00:53:45.391 --> 00:53:47.999
I could completely understand, you know.

00:53:47.999 --> 00:53:51.428
There is no this is the right way.

00:53:51.428 --> 00:53:52.261
No.

00:53:53.636 --> 00:53:55.719
Everybody has his way of-

00:53:55.719 --> 00:53:56.797
- [Connor] Doing it, right?

00:53:56.797 --> 00:53:57.630
- Doing it.

00:53:57.630 --> 00:53:59.794
And in my case, I wanted to work

